#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:2
msgid "Timer string format"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:4
msgid ""
"Timer strings are used in both `refresh.timer` stetting and timer services "
"as the `timer: ..` app property. See https://forum.snapcraft.io/t/refresh-"
"scheduling-on-specific-days-of-the-month/1239/6 for details on how the "
"syntax conceived and evolved over time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:6
msgid "Syntax"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:8
msgid ""
"eventlist = eventset *( \",,\" eventset )\n"
"eventset = wdaylist / timelist / wdaylist \",\" timelist\n"
"\n"
"wdaylist = wdayset *( \",\" wdayset )\n"
"wdayset = wday / wdaynumber / wdayspan\n"
"wday =  ( \"mon\" / \"tue\" / \"wed\" / \"thu\" / \"fri\" / \"sat\" / "
"\"sun\" )\n"
"wdaynumber =  ( \"sun\" / \"mon\" / \"tue\" / \"wed\" / \"thu\" / \"fri\" / "
"\"sat\" ) DIGIT\n"
"wdayspan = wday \"-\" wday / wdaynumber \"-\" wday / wday \"-\" wdaynumber\n"
"wspec = ( \"1\" / \"2\" / \"3\" / \"4\" / \"5\" )\n"
"\n"
"timelist = timeset *( \",\" timeset )\n"
"timeset = time / timespan\n"
"time = 2DIGIT \":\" 2DIGIT\n"
"timespan = time ( \"-\" / \"~\" ) time [ \"/\" count ]\n"
"count = n*DIGIT\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:25
msgid "Clock times are always specified in 24H format."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:27
msgid "Examples"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:29
msgid "`00:00-24:00/24`</br>  Every hour on the hour"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:29
#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:32
#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:35
#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:38
#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:41
#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:46
#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:49
#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:52
#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:55
#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:58
#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:61
#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:64
#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:67
msgid "</br>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:32
msgid "`00:00-24:00/48`</br> Every 30 minutes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:35
msgid "`00:00-24:00/96`</br> Every 15 minutes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:38
msgid "`12:00-13:00/12`</br>  Every 5 minutes from 12:00 to 13:10"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:41
msgid "`23:00`</br>  Every day at 23:00"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:44
msgid "More specific timer examples:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:46
msgid "`mon,10:00,,fri,15:00`</br>  Mondays at 10:00, Fridays at 15:10"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:49
msgid ""
"`mon,fri,10:00,15:00`</br> Mondays at 10:00 and 15:00, Fridays at 10:00 and "
"15:00"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:52
msgid ""
"`mon-wed,fri,9:00-11:00/2`</br> Monday to Wednesday and on Friday, twice "
"between 9:00 and 11:00"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:55
msgid ""
"`mon,9:00~11:00,,wed,22:00~23:00`</br> Mondays, some time between 9:00 and "
"11:00, and on Wednesdays, some time between 22:00 and 23:00"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:58
msgid "`mon,wed`</br> Monday and on Wednesday, at 0:00"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:61
msgid ""
"`mon2-wed2,23:00-24:00` (**DEPRECATED** in snapd _2.43+_)</br> Monday "
"through Wednesday on the 2nd week of the month, between 23:00 and 24:00"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:64
msgid ""
"`mon2-wed,23:00-24:00` (from snapd versions _2.43+_)</br> 2nd Monday of the "
"month through the following Wednesday, between 23:00 and 24:00"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:67
msgid ""
"`fri5,23:00-01:00`</br> Last Friday of the month, from 23:00 to 1:00 the "
"next day. Even in months with 4 Fridays, this schedule will still trigger on "
"the last Friday."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:70
msgid "Semantics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:72
msgid ""
"A timer string is composed of one or more event sets, which are combined by "
"using commas (`,,`) as separators."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:74
msgid ""
"Each event set defines the weekdays and the time windows in which events may "
"occur. The next event will be scheduled inside the soonest opportunity that "
"matches both one of the provided weekdays and one of the provided time "
"windows. If no weekdays are provided, the default is every day. If no time "
"windows are provided, the default is an arbitrary time in the day."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:76
msgid "For example, consider the timer:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:78
msgid "mon,fri,10:00,15:00\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:80
msgid "Assuming today is Sunday, the next 5 events are, in order:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:82
msgid ""
"Monday 10:00\n"
"Monday 15:00\n"
"Friday 10:00\n"
"Friday 15:00\n"
"Monday 10:00\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:88
msgid "Consider the following timer:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:90
msgid "mon,10:00,,fri,15:00\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:92
msgid "The next 3 events in this case are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:94
msgid ""
"Monday 10am\n"
"Friday 15pm\n"
"Monday 10am\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:98
msgid ""
"All of these examples work on a weekly basis, but certain events are better "
"scheduled on a monthly basis. To support that, weekdays may be suffixed by "
"`wspec` entry that defines the week number inside the month. As an example, "
"the following timer defines two events every month, on the first and third "
"Mondays at 15:00:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:100
msgid "mon1,mon3,15:00\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:102
msgid ""
"As a special case, the 5th week is considered the last one to hold the given "
"day, so that specifying an event on the last Friday of the month, for "
"instance, is done simply as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:104
msgid "fri5\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:106
msgid ""
"In addition to specifying precise weekdays, an interval may be used to "
"define a larger span:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:108
msgid "mon-fri,15:00\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:110
msgid ""
"This represents an event per day at 15:00, Monday through Friday, every week."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:112
msgid ""
"The same interval syntax also works to define time spans, but the meaning is "
"slightly different. For instance, consider this time span:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:114
msgid "mon,14:00-16:00\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:116
msgid ""
"It defines an event every Monday that will take place at the earliest chance "
"between 14:100 and 16:00."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:118
msgid ""
"Weekday spans define an event **every day** within the span. In contrast, "
"time spans define a **single event** inside the defined span."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:120
msgid ""
"That latter aspect may be changed via an explicit divisor, which may be "
"specified as a `count`. For instance, consider this time span:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:122
msgid "8:00-16:00/2\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:124
msgid ""
"This represents two events every day, one in the morning between 8:00 and "
"12:00, and another one between 12:00 and 16:00."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:126
msgid "While the following represents an hourly event, every day of the week:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:128
msgid "0:00-24:00/24\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:130
msgid ""
"All of the time spans defined so far work similarly in the sense that the "
"start of the span defines the earliest chance in which the event may start, "
"and the end of the span defines the latest chance for the event to have "
"started. For various reasons, though, it’s often useful to introduce some "
"level of randomization inside the time span so that events won’t all start "
"at exactly the same time. This may be achieved by replacing the time span "
"dash character (`-`) by a tilde (`~`). Consider the following time span:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:132
msgid "0:00~24:00/4\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:134
msgid ""
"It represents 4 events that will take place at a random time inside time "
"windows of 6 hours each."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:136
msgid ""
"Week spans that need to start or end during a specific week in the month can "
"be defined by appending the week number to either the start or end of a week "
"span. Consider the following schedules:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:138
msgid ""
"mon1-fri\n"
"mon-fri1\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:141
msgid ""
"The first example describes a week span that starts on the first Monday of "
"the month and ends on the following Friday, while the second example defines "
"a week span that starts on the Monday _before_ the first Friday of the "
"month, which is when the span ends."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:143
msgid "Consider the following calendar months of July and August 2019:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:145
msgid ""
"        July                  August       \n"
"Su Mo Tu We Th Fr Sa   Su Mo Tu We Th Fr Sa\n"
"    1  2  3  4  5  6                1  2  3\n"
" 7  8  9 10 11 12 13    4  5  6  7  8  9 10\n"
"14 15 16 17 18 19 20   11 12 13 14 15 16 17\n"
"21 22 23 24 25 26 27   18 19 20 21 22 23 24\n"
"28 29 30 31            25 26 27 28 29 30 31\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/timer-string-format.md:155
msgid ""
"In the above context, `mon1-fri` corresponds describes a span from 5th of "
"August to the 9th of August, while `mon-fri1` covers 29th of July until 2nd "
"of August."
msgstr ""
